TN-Eval  (rubric, SOAP prompt, scoring prompts)
  Apache License 2.0 -- https://github.com/amazon-science/TN-Eval

  Note: the licence covers the CODE repository, which is where the prompts and
  the rubric come from. The notes and human annotations live in a separate
  repository, TN-Eval-Data, which publishes no licence -- verified 2026-08-24.
  They are fetched at run time and never redistributed here.
  Shah, Raj Sanjay; Xu, Lei; Liu, Qianchu; Burnsky, Jon; Bertagnolli, Drew;
  Shivade, Chaitanya. "TN-Eval: Rubric and Evaluation Protocols for Measuring
  the Quality of Behavioral Therapy Notes." ACL 2025, Industry Track.

  This repository reproduces TN-Eval's rubric text, SOAP generation prompt and
  scoring prompts. They are used under the Apache-2.0 licence, unmodified in
  wording, with the model backend replaced (AWS Bedrock -> OpenAI-compatible).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
PDSQI-9  (clinical note quality instrument, the pdsqi-soap track)
  CC BY 4.0 -- https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.08977
  Croxford, Emma; Gao, Yanjun; Pellegrino, Nicholas; et al. "Development and
  Validation of the Provider Documentation Summarization Quality Instrument for
  Large Language Models." 2025.

  Itself an adaptation of the Physician Documentation Quality Instrument
  (PDQI-9): Stetson, Peter D.; Bakken, Suzanne; Wrenn, Jesse O.; Siegler,
  Eugenia L. "Assessing Electronic Note Quality Using the Physician
  Documentation Quality Instrument (PDQI-9)." Appl Clin Inform 2012,
  PMC3347480.

  CC BY 4.0 permits reproduction and adaptation with attribution. This
  repository reproduces eight of the nine attributes with their published
  questions and 1-5 anchors, and states its three changes in
  `src/tnb/scoring/pdsqi.py`: the "Cited" attribute is dropped (a note written
  from one transcript has no source documents to cite), the words "summary" and
  "source notes" are replaced by "note" and "session transcript", and the
  binary stigmatizing-language item is reported inverted so that every column in
  a table is better when higher. The judge is asked the published question.

